# Pull Request

## What changed and why

Issue #439, PR 2: reproducible capacity scenarios. PR 1 (#559) added `greengateway_database_pool_acquire_seconds` and counted each path's checkouts. Until now nothing ran those paths under load, so there was no evidence for how pool waits, refusals and database activity change with replica count, pool ceiling and request mix.

- **The matrix.** `gateway/tests/ha/capacity.rs` is a module of the existing `ha_performance` target. It reuses that target's harness, cluster options, fixtures and nearest-rank quantiles. A separate target would also have to become a release-gate leg under `release_gate_workflow.rs`. The matrix has nine cells:
  - two replica counts (2, 3), crossed with two pool ceilings (2, 10, where 10 is the product default) and two request mixes;
  - `jwt_proxy`: a proxied `GET` with a `jti`-bearing JWT;
  - `mixed_credentials`: six JWT `GET`s, three service-token `GET`s and one admin policy read in every ten requests;
  - `r2-p10-mixed_credentials-contention`: the mixed load, plus policy commits on alternating replicas (each waits for every replica to compile it, at most 10 s, then pauses 250 ms) and a 200-event `http.request_observed` batch on the audit stream every second for the discovery projector.

  Every cell starts on a fresh cluster and database. The load is 24 closed-loop workers spread evenly over the replicas, a 2 s discarded warm-up and a 20 s measured window.
- **What each cell records.** Everything below is per cell:
  - **Requests:** completed, throughput, status counts, client timeouts, `rate_503` and `rate_client_timeout`, and p50/p95/p99/max latency, overall, for served requests only and per request kind.
  - **Acquisition series:** per replica, by `operation_class` and `error_class`, with exact count and sum deltas and the exporter's quantiles. Deployment-wide counts cover all 56 class and outcome pairs, so a zero `cancelled` is stated explicitly.
  - **Pool:** gauges sampled every 500 ms, and the pool-timeout and audit-drop deltas.
  - **Database:** `pg_stat_activity` and `pg_locks` counts sampled every 500 ms, lock-wait maxima and `pg_stat_database` deltas.
  - **Contention cell only:** commit latency, revision convergence (from commit return until every replica's `greengateway_security_revision_compiled` reaches the committed revision), and projector checkpoint progress.
- **Gate: harness validity only.** The test fails only when a cell's evidence is incomplete, never on a latency, refusal rate or wait. A cell is incomplete if it has any of the following:
  - fewer requests than workers, or no request served at all;
  - a status other than `200`/`503`;
  - client transport errors;
  - a request kind with no completions;
  - a replica with no scraped `revision_check` acquisitions (any outcome, so a replica whose reads were all cancelled still counts), or a zero pool `size` gauge;
  - an exercised class missing from the counts;
  - fewer than three database samples;
  - in the contention cell, no converged commit or an idle projector.

  An incomplete cell is recorded with fixed reason codes, and the test fails after the whole matrix has run. No capacity budget is introduced.
- **One client address per worker.** The global limiter's shared bucket is keyed by client address. On a single address, the first local run queued every request on one bucket row and produced `429`s. Each worker now sends its own TEST-NET-2 `X-Forwarded-For`, with `TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS` restricted to `127.0.0.1/32` in the test cluster only.
- **Sanitized, bounded evidence.** The report carries no URL, port, DSN, role or database name, token, `jti`, request ID, statement text or error message. Labels are read only through the fixed vocabularies; anything else marks the cell incomplete. `scripts/check-test-evidence.py capacity` re-checks the following, then writes `ha-capacity.json` from an allowlist:
  - vocabularies, count consistency and quantile order. Nearest-rank latencies (requests, commits, convergence) must satisfy p50 <= p95 <= p99 <= max. The exporter's acquisition quantiles are DDSketch bucket representatives while its max is exact, so a p99 sharing the max's bucket can sit just above it; for those rows only p50 <= p95 <= p99 is required;
  - completeness, and a measured window at least as long as `method.measured_seconds`;
  - the matrix shape: at least two replica counts by two pool ceilings by both mixes, plus one contention cell that runs the mixed load on a configuration a plain cell also ran.

  A field added to the producer by mistake does not reach the `ha-capacity` artifact. The result is about 140 KB for nine cells.
- **Workflow.** `nightly-performance.yml` stays scheduled or manual, and adds no PR check. Changes:
  - the budget step gets `id: budgets` and `--skip capacity::`;
  - a new `capacity` step runs `--exact` the one matrix test, with its own anti-vacuity guard (exactly one test, more than 60 s) and `timeout-minutes: 30`, inside the job's 90;
  - it runs whenever the budget step ran, pass or fail;
  - validation and upload follow `docs/deployment/test-evidence.md`: `always() && steps.capacity.outcome == 'success'`, then a strict `if-no-files-found: error` upload of `ha-capacity` (90 days);
  - when the producer did not succeed, or the validator (`id: capacity_evidence`) rejected its report, the producer's own report is kept best-effort as `ha-capacity-diagnostics`, so a red night always leaves evidence; that file is the producer's sanitized output, not the allowlisted rebuild;
  - the budget baseline is now the last successful scheduled run on `main` (`branch=main&event=schedule`), so a manual run of a branch never becomes the next night's comparison.
- **Docs.**
  - `authority-cost.md` gains "The capacity matrix". It lists the cells and what each field is, covers how to read one axis at a time, the rolling-window, censoring and sketch-quantile caveats, `lwlock_waiting` vs lock waits, and that `longest_lock_wait_ms` is an upper bound. It says exactly what the gate checks, and how to collect the same numbers in a deployment: `/metrics` needs no credential, `pg_locks` and `pg_stat_database` are readable by any role, the runtime role already sees its own sessions' state and wait type, and other roles' session details in `pg_stat_activity` need a separate `pg_monitor` monitoring role, never the runtime role. It also describes how the report feeds the #439 PR 3 sizing guard.
  - `pool-sizing.md` names the three numbers that matter for sizing and says they are evidence, not limits.
  - `test-evidence.md` gains a row for the new producer.
  - `performance.rs`'s module notes point at the matrix.

## Validation

- `scripts/test_security_gates.py`: 38 tests pass. The new ones cover the validator on complete, incomplete, missing, shrunken, hostile-label, inconsistent and contention-less reports, an exporter-shaped single-sample acquisition row (p50 = p95 = p99 just above max, accepted), a replica whose revision reads were all cancelled (accepted), unserved, short and unread-gauge cells (rejected), and the nightly workflow contract.
- The capacity module's database-free tests pass, and `cargo clippy --test ha_performance -- -D warnings` and `cargo fmt --check` are clean. The tests cover the exposition parser and its vocabularies, label escapes, the mix rotation, the matrix shape, and request- and pool-evidence reason codes, and they run on every PR through `cargo test --workspace`.
- I ran the full matrix locally against a disposable PostgreSQL 16 container (Windows, 5 s windows). All nine cells were complete, and the validator accepted the real report. That machine's numbers are not meaningful. The nightly run on this branch is the first real measurement.

